

================================================================================
Benchmark: torch.cat vs direct copy for MLA k_nope/k_pe concatenation
================================================================================
Tensor shapes: k_nope=[B, 128, 128], k_pe=[B, 1, 64]
dtype: bfloat16

  Batch Size |   cat (ms) |  direct (ms) |  Speedup |  Reduction
----------------------------------------------------------------------
          32 |      0.125 |        0.230 |    0.54x |     -84.6%
          64 |      0.250 |        0.218 |    1.15x |      13.0%
         128 |      0.194 |        0.230 |    0.84x |     -18.5%
         256 |      0.368 |        0.294 |    1.25x |      20.2%
         512 |      0.725 |        0.560 |    1.30x |      22.8%
        1024 |      1.439 |        1.110 |    1.30x |      22.9%
        2048 |      2.849 |        2.200 |    1.30x |      22.8%
        4096 |      5.559 |        4.545 |    1.22x |      18.2%
        8192 |     11.155 |        9.072 |    1.23x |      18.7%
       16384 |     22.241 |       18.112 |    1.23x |      18.6%
       32768 |     44.465 |       36.186 |    1.23x |      18.6%
       65536 |     88.867 |       72.342 |    1.23x |      18.6%
================================================================================

Speedup summary:
  Min:  0.54x
  Max:  1.30x
  Mean: 1.15x

Conclusion:
  - Direct copy becomes beneficial at batch size >= 64
  - For batch sizes >= 512: avg speedup = 1.25x
  - MLA prefill typically uses large batches, so optimization is effective


